//? Title: Closest Pair: An Auditable All-Pairs Search
//? Subtitle: Original point identities, actual improvements, extreme scales and computed-distance ties
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Given planar point records, identify the two distinct input records with the
smallest Euclidean separation. Duplicate locations, tied distances and extreme
coordinate scales make even a short scan worth specifying carefully. We derive
the all-pairs invariant, inspect actual incumbent improvements, retain the
original seeded cloud with its planted pair, and measure exact pair work.
Canonical C++ now validates finite coordinates, compares widened distances,
returns original indices and offers a bounded actual-improvement trace. A
rounding counterexample distinguishes the minimum computed distance from an
exact geometric predicate.
)

\toc

> Four points and three actual improvements

Take points 0=(0,0), 1=(3,4), 2=(0,1), 3=(0.25,1). Visit pairs in original
index order: (0,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (1,3), (2,3). The first distance,
five, initializes the incumbent. Visit two reduces it to one, and visit six
reduces it to 0.25. These three states are captured by the executing kernel;
the other visits still occur but do not improve the incumbent.

The native method diagram uses one coordinate scale on both axes. Coloured
segments show the three actual incumbent pairs, and the table gives their
one-based pair-visit ordinals. Point labels identify input records, not a
coordinate-sorted permutation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/closest_pair_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> points{{0,0},{3,4},{0,1},{0.25,1}};
  const auto trace=dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace(points);
  const std::vector<dm::RGB> colours{{37,99,235},{234,88,12},{220,38,38}};
  dm::graphics::Diagram figure(90,1100);
  std::print("| actual pair visit | new incumbent pair | measured distance |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<trace.improvements.size();++k) {
    const auto& step=trace.improvements[k];const auto a=points[step.first],b=points[step.second];
    std::print("| {} | ({},{}) | {:.6f} |\n",step.comparison,step.first,step.second,step.distance);
    dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color=colours[k];stroke.width=2.5;
    figure.path({{a.x,a.y},{b.x,b.y}},stroke)
          .text({6,4-1.3*k},std::format("visit {}: pair ({},{}), d={:.2f}",step.comparison,step.first,step.second,step.distance),20,colours[k]);
  }
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<points.size();++i) {
    const auto p=points[i];figure.ellipse({p.x,p.y},0.045,0.045,{15,23,42});
    const auto label=i==0?dm::graphics::DiagramPoint{-0.25,-0.25}:i==1?dm::graphics::DiagramPoint{3.2,4.15}:i==2?dm::graphics::DiagramPoint{-0.25,1}:dm::graphics::DiagramPoint{0.55,1};
    figure.text(label,std::to_string(i),20);
  }
  figure.text({3.8,5.5},"A strict incumbent change records actual scan state",23)
        .text({3.8,-1.4},std::format("{} pairs evaluated; final pair ({},{}), distance {}",trace.work.pair_visits,trace.result.first,trace.result.second,trace.result.distance),20)
        .save_svg("build/closest_pair_method.svg");
  if(trace.result.first!=2 || trace.result.second!=3 || trace.result.distance!=0.25L)return 1;
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual pair visit | new incumbent pair | measured distance |
| ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 1 | (0,1) | 5.000000 |
| 2 | (0,2) | 1.000000 |
| 6 | (2,3) | 0.250000 |
\image(figures/closest_pair_method.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The incumbent changes at visits one, two and six. Every unordered pair is still evaluated.)
\alttext(Four labelled points show the blue initial pair from zero to one, orange improved pair from zero to two, and short red final pair from two to three. The legend gives distances five, one and 0.25.)

> What the scan minimizes

For distinct input positions i<j, define

$$
d_{ij}=\sqrt{(x_i-x_j)^2+(y_i-y_j)^2}.
$$
\alttext(Euclidean distance is the square root of the sum of the squared coordinate differences.)

Distinct records may have equal coordinates, yielding distance zero. The search
does not deduplicate or sort them. It initializes from the first pair and
replaces the incumbent only on a strict improvement. Equal computed distances
therefore retain the lexicographically first original index pair.

After processing the first k candidate pairs, the incumbent is the first pair
attaining their minimum computed distance. Initialization establishes this
invariant; comparing the next finite value either preserves that incumbent or
replaces it with a strict smaller value. Induction gives the minimum of all
computed pair distances at termination. That proof does not make floating
distance evaluations exact in real arithmetic.

The implementation forms widened coordinate differences and calls widened
hypot for every pair. The old report claimed squared-distance comparisons and
one final square root, but the old code actually called distance for every pair
through a double squared-distance expression. Large squares could overflow,
tiny squares could underflow, and nonfinite points were not rejected. Avoiding
those intermediate squares fixes those failures without changing the quadratic
search strategy. The separate legacy geometry squared_distance helper is not
an exact-arithmetic oracle for this search.

All coordinates validate before a positive complete pair-work cap. Fewer than
two points reject, including empty input. The indexed result retains widened
distance and copied points; the existing closest_pair call retains its original
result layout and returns a checked finite-double distance. An out-of-range
selected legacy distance throws. Both use one kernel, and tracing records
actual strict changes without replay.

> The original seeded cloud and planted pair

The original example generated sixty MT19937 points in [0,10)² with seed
424242, then appended (5,5) and (5.03,4.98). The reusable native rectangle
generator preserves that draw order: x then y for each record. It uses local
engine state and standard uniform-real distributions. The engine is specified,
but distribution-to-floating mappings can differ between standard libraries;
the seed is a reproducibility control within the selected implementation, not
a portable byte-level dataset specification.

For this checked execution, records 60 and 61 are the selected pair among all
62 records, after 1,891 pair evaluations. Planting a pair does not guarantee
that it beats every background pair under arbitrary seeds or sample counts;
the search measures that fact here. There is no external dataset to download.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/closest_pair_cloud.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto points=dm::geometry::uniform_rectangle_points(60,424242);
  points.push_back({5,5});points.push_back({5.03,4.98});
  const auto trace=dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace(points);const auto& result=trace.result;
  std::print("| points | selected input indices | measured distance | pair visits | actual improvements |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| {} | ({},{}) | {:.12f} | {} | {} |\n",points.size(),result.first,result.second,result.distance,trace.work.pair_visits,trace.improvements.size());
  std::vector<double> x,y;for(const auto p:points){x.push_back(p.x);y.push_back(p.y);}
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(x,y,"cloud",{100,116,139},4)
      .line({result.a.x,result.b.x},{result.a.y,result.b.y},"winner",{220,38,38},2)
      .points({result.a.x,result.b.x},{result.a.y,result.b.y},"winner",{220,38,38},7)
      .legend({"all records","closest pair"},{{100,116,139},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Original seeded cloud: 62 records, one measured closest pair")
      .x_label("x coordinate").y_label("y coordinate")
      .x_limits(0,10).y_limits(0,11).save_svg("build/closest_pair_cloud.svg");
  if(result.first!=60 || result.second!=61)return 1;
}
```
