> Caps, extreme scales and a genuine rounded tie

The positive pair cap checks the complete forecast after validating every
coordinate and before any pair execution. The positive improvement cap applies
only to traces and is enforced online against actual improvements, including
initialization. It does not reserve space for every pair or reject a one-record
trace merely because many pairs exist. Zero caps mean unlimited. Any failure
returns no partial result or trace. Records and selected point copies own their
storage independently of the input.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<dm::geometry::Point2D> points{{0,0},{3,4},{0,1},{0.25,1}};
  dm::geometry::ClosestPairOptions options;options.max_pair_visits=6;options.max_improvements=3;
  const auto exact=dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace(points,options);
  bool pair_rejected=false,record_rejected=false,invalid_first=false,legacy_overflow=false;
  options.max_pair_visits=5;
  try { (void)dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace(points,options); }catch(const std::length_error&) { pair_rejected=true; }
  options.max_pair_visits=6;options.max_improvements=2;
  try { (void)dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace(points,options); }catch(const std::length_error&) { record_rejected=true; }
  const auto unrecorded=dm::geometry::closest_pair_indexed(points,options);
  options.max_pair_visits=1;
  try { (void)dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace({{0,0},{0,0},{std::numeric_limits<double>::quiet_NaN(),0}},options); }catch(const std::invalid_argument&) { invalid_first=true; }
  std::print("| checked limit | expected outcome observed |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| pair cap one below six | {} |\n| actual trace cap one below three | {} |\n| invalid trailing point before pair cap | {} |\n| untraced call ignores trace cap | {} |\n",pair_rejected,record_rejected,invalid_first,unrecorded.distance==0.25L);
  std::print("\n| coordinate scale | selected pair | measured distance |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: |\n");
  for(const double scale:{1e-200,1e200}) {
    const auto r=dm::geometry::closest_pair_indexed({{0,0},{3*scale,4*scale},{0,scale}});
    std::print("| {:.0e} | ({},{}) | {:.9e} |\n",scale,r.first,r.second,r.distance);
    if(r.first!=0 || r.second!=2)return 1;
  }
  const double big=std::numeric_limits<double>::max();
  const auto wide=dm::geometry::closest_pair_indexed({{-big,0},{big,0}});
  try { (void)dm::geometry::closest_pair({{-big,0},{big,0}}); }catch(const std::overflow_error&) { legacy_overflow=true; }
  std::print("\nWidened selected distance exceeds double: {}; legacy conversion throws: {}.\n",wide.distance>big,legacy_overflow);
  const double epsilon=std::ldexp(1.0,-std::numeric_limits<long double>::digits);
  const auto rounded=dm::geometry::closest_pair_trace({{0,0},{1,epsilon},{10,0},{11,0}});
  std::print("epsilon={:.9e}; selected pair ({},{}); rounded distance {}; equal-incumbent candidates {}.\n",epsilon,rounded.result.first,rounded.result.second,rounded.result.distance,rounded.work.ties);
  if(!pair_rejected || !record_rejected || !invalid_first || !legacy_overflow || exact.improvements.size()!=3)return 1;
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| checked limit | expected outcome observed |
| :--- | :--- |
| pair cap one below six | true |
| actual trace cap one below three | true |
| invalid trailing point before pair cap | true |
| untraced call ignores trace cap | true |

| coordinate scale | selected pair | measured distance |
| ---: | :--- | ---: |
| 1e-200 | (0,2) | 1.000000000e-200 |
| 1e+200 | (0,2) | 1.000000000e+200 |

Widened selected distance exceeds double: true; legacy conversion throws: true.
epsilon=5.421010862e-20; selected pair (0,1); rounded distance 1; equal-incumbent candidates 1.
// result_end

In the last construction ε is positive and exactly representable. Pair (0,1)
has true distance √(1+ε²)>1; pair (2,3) has true distance exactly one. Their
widened hypot values both round to one on this platform, so the first measured
tie retains (0,1). Thus the scan correctly implements its computed-distance
contract while returning a pair that is not the exact-real closest pair.
Widening is valuable range protection, not arbitrary precision or a certified
geometric comparison.

Seventeen new native tests pass address and undefined-behaviour sanitizers.
Exact integer squared-distance oracles check every improvement, index and named
counter for all 6,561 ordered four-record tuples on a 3×3 grid, all 502
nontrivial grid subsets, and 1,000 seeded integer clouds. Another 720 input
permutations and exactly representable similarity transforms check tie identity.
Cases also cover smallest subnormal distances, huge irrelevant pairs, widened
selected distances beyond double, full validation before limits, online record
caps, zero-distance scans, generator bounds/reproducibility and owned copies.
The original sixty draws and planted pair are checked directly against the
original MT19937 construction. Existing legacy and formatting regressions pass.

Python's existing legacy binding retains its result shape and updated help.
Its scoped SWIG rule maps invalid input to ValueError and selected-distance
overflow to OverflowError. A targeted generated-header SWIG probe runs the
binding tests for loading, owned results, ties, extreme scales and those errors;
this is separate from a full installed-package build. The indexed and trace
APIs demonstrated in this report are native C++ APIs.

> What the analysis establishes

Every unordered candidate contributes to a precisely counted all-pairs scan.
The native trace explains how the minimum computed distance emerges, and
original indices preserve duplicate and tie identities. The original seeded
cloud is now a complete executable example with a readable geometric zoom.
Widened arithmetic avoids false squared-distance zero/infinity results, while
the explicit rounded-tie construction sets the limit of the correctness claim.
Large-scale or exact-predicate requirements need an appropriate different
algorithm or arithmetic contract.

> References

- M. I. Shamos and D. Hoey (1975), *Closest-point problems*, 16th IEEE Symposium
  on Foundations of Computer Science, 151–162,
  https://doi.org/10.1109/SFCS.1975.8. Background for faster closest-point methods;
  this report's kernel deliberately enumerates all unordered pairs.
- *Divide and Conquer*, closest-pair reading hosted by Carnegie Mellon:
  https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~avrim/451f12/lectures/lect1120-closest-pair2.pdf.
  The maintained strip merge explains the O(n log n) alternative, not this API.
- Canonical contracts: include/datamunge/geometry/closest_pair.hpp,
  include/datamunge/geometry/closest_pair_search.hpp and
  src/datamunge/geometry/closest_pair.cpp. Complete editable C++ programs above
  generate every figure as a local file.
